    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d188">

                <pb facs="0304" n="234" sameAs="frus:frus1862/pg234"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Adams.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 396.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1862-11-10"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >November</hi> 10, 1862.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatch of the 23d of October (No.
                    244) has been received. The President regrets that her Majesty’s government has
                    not more favorably considered our complaints against the violations of municipal
                    and international law, committed by British subjects under the British flag, in
                    the case of the <pb facs="0305" n="235" xml:id="pg235"/> steamer “290,” or
                    “Alabama.” It is to be apprehended that attempts by the same and similar vessels
                    to repeat the same injuries will ultimately require a more deliberate
                    consideration of the subject than the government now seems willing to
                    accord.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Francis Adams,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
